
Question 1: Your cluster has 2 nodes. The team wants certain pods to run only on a specific node — like pinning a workload to a dedicated worker.
Tasks:
-> Check your existing nodes and their labels using kubectl get nodes --show-labels
-> Label node-1 with hardware=standard and node-2 with hardware=high-memory
-> Create a pod named data-processor using image nginx with nodeSelector targeting hardware=high-memory
-> Verify it landed on node-2 using kubectl get pods -o wide
-> Create a second pod named web-server targeting hardware=standard — verify it lands on node-1
-> Now create a pod with nodeSelector: hardware=gpu (label that doesn't exist on any node) — observe what happens and explain why it stays Pending
 


Question 2: You have 2 nodes — one labeled as zone=east and one as zone=west. You need to deploy workloads with both hard and soft placement rules.

Tasks:
-> Label node-1 as zone=east and disk=ssd
-> Label node-2 as zone=west and disk=hdd
-> Create a deployment named web-app with 4 replicas using requiredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution — pod must run in zone=east only
-> Observe — all 4 replicas land on node-1 since node-2 doesn't match the required rule
-> Now create another deployment named cache-app with preferredDuringSchedulingIgnoredDuringExecution preferring disk=ssd with weight 80
-> Observe how pods distribute across both nodes since it is a preference not a hard rule
-> Remove the zone=east label from node-1 while web-app pods are running — explain what IgnoredDuringExecution means by observing that existing pods are not evicted
-> Try scheduling a new pod with the required rule — observe it goes Pending now


Question 3: You want node-2 reserved exclusively for production critical workloads. Regular pods should never land there unless they explicitly tolerate it.

Tasks:
-> Taint node-2 with env=prod:NoSchedule
-> Deploy a regular nginx deployment with 6 replicas — confirm all pods land only on node-1
-> Create a pod named prod-app with the correct toleration for env=prod:NoSchedule — verify it can be scheduled on node-2
-> Now change the taint on node-2 to env=prod:NoExecute
-> Observe what happens to existing pods running on node-2 that don't have the toleration — they get evicted
-> Add tolerationSeconds: 120 to a pod tolerating the NoExecute taint — explain the delayed eviction behavior
-> Remove the taint from node-2 completely — observe pods reschedule freely again
 

############ Example Topology Spread Constraint ###########
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
 
metadata:
  name: dep1
 
spec:
  replicas: 6
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: webserver

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: webserver

    spec:
      topologySpreadConstraints:
        - maxSkew: 2
          topologyKey: kubernetes.io/hostname
          whenUnsatisfiable: DoNotSchedule
          labelSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: webserver

      containers:
        - name: cont1
          image: httpd:2.4

 
